    "text": "A refrigerator keeps food cold by moving heat from inside to outside, like carrying warmth away in a bucket. A special liquid called refrigerant flows through pipes. First, it absorbs heat from inside the fridge and turns into a gas. Then a compressor squeezes that gas, making it hot. Next, the hot gas flows through coils on the back or bottom, where it releases heat into your kitchen. As it cools, it turns back into a liquid. Finally, it flows back inside to soak up more heat. This cycle repeats over and over, so the inside stays cold while the outside gets the warmth."
